tel 1.0

base denominative
import soundness

info   ordinals, intervals and confined indexes: counting and indexing by proof rather than by integer
detail
    _Denominative_ is how Soundness counts and indexes. Its central type is `Ordinal`, a
    position — first, second, third — which is a different type from `Int` altogether, so an
    off-by-one error is a type error: there is no zeroth ordinal, the first is `Prim`, then
    `Sec`, `Ter`, `Quat`, `Quin`, `Sen` and `Sept`, and a number crosses into an ordinal only by
    saying which convention it came from (`0.z` reads a zero-based index, `1.u` a one-based
    count; both are `Prim`). A range of ordinals is an `Interval` (`Ter thru Sen`, `Ter till
    Sen`, `Ter span 3`), and a located range of characters in a source is a `Span`.

    The second idea is the *confined* ordinal, `Ordinal in xs.type`: an ordinal whose type
    records the collection it has been proved to index. Only a combinator which has already
    established the bound can mint one — `xs.extent` (the whole range, branded), `ordinal
    .within(xs)`, `xs.spot(p)`, `xs.prefix(p)`, rudiments' `confine` and `pick` — and reading
    with one goes through a total `apply` which yields a bare element, where an unproven index
    yields an `Optional`. A counting loop is therefore written without a counter and without a
    bounds check on either side:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val array = Array(10, 20, 30)
    var total = 0
    array.extent.each { ordinal => total += array(ordinal) }   // total is 60; array(ordinal) is an Int, not an Optional
    ```

    The typeclasses which make a value countable and indexable live here: `Countable` (its
    `size`, and the ordinals derived from it: `gamut`, `extent`, `limit`, `ult`, `pen`, `ant`),
    `Vacuiscible` (emptiness alone, `nil`, O(1) everywhere), `Applicable` (`contains` and
    `access` by an index or key, behind rudiments' `apply`, `at`, `defines`, `confine` and
    `pick`), `Definable` and `Omissible` (the copy-on-write `define` and `omit`), and
    `Terminable` and `Truncable` (`last` and `lead`). Their instances cover the frozen
    `Array[e]^{}` and `Data`, `Sequence`, `Text`, `Set`, `Map`, `Ledger`, the stdlib
    `IndexedSeq` and a few mutable buffers — and, behind an acknowledgement, `List` and `Chain`.

    That acknowledgement is the third idea. An operation is *dysasymptotic* when its cost grows
    with data outside its subject: `size` is free on a `Sequence` but a traversal on a `List`,
    `last` on a `List` walks to its end, `at` on a `List` walks from its head, and `size` on a
    `Chain` forces the whole stream and may never return. Such instances take a plain marker
    type in a `using` position — `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize`, `LinearAccess`, `UnboundedSize`,
    `LinearScan` — and a file opts in by importing the matching given from the
    `dysasymptotics` package *by name* (`import dysasymptotics.linearSize`; a wildcard import
    does not bring givens into scope), ideally in the block that accepts the cost, with a
    comment saying why it is acceptable. Every acknowledgement is greppable, and `nil` is
    never gated. A `.stdlib.length` which dodges the gate hides the cost; open the gate instead.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A list's size is a traversal, so it is not simply available
      code
          locally:
            val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
            numbers.size   // does not compile
    frame
      label Acknowledge the linear cost, by name
      code
          locally:
            import dysasymptotics.linearSize   // the lists here are short
            val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
            numbers.size   // 3
    frame
      label Positional access is a different acknowledgement
      code
          locally:
            import dysasymptotics.{linearSize, linearAccess}
            val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
            numbers.size        // 3
            numbers.at(Ter)     // 2
            numbers.last        // 2
    ##
    ```

    The library is one source set, `denominative.core`, compiled for the JVM, Scala.js and
    Scala Native, sitting just above vacuous and prepositional and below rudiments: the
    `Optional`-returning reads (`at`, `apply`, `pick`, `confine`, `head`, `last`) are rudiments'
    and concordance's extensions over these typeclasses. Everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella, where the `dysasymptotics` and `ordinalTextualizables` choice
    packages merge with the umbrella's own; `thru`, `till` and `tabulate` are re-declared
    there (and `each` in rudiments' umbrella file) rather than exported, since an export
    forwards only one overload of an extension and both the plain and the confined overload
    must be reachable. A file which
    imports `denominative.*` directly must not also import `rudiments.*` wholesale for the
    same names.

refer denominative.Ordinal
refer denominative.Interval
refer denominative.Countable
refer denominative.Applicable
refer denominative.dysasymptotics

entry .Prim   the first ordinal
    `Ordinal.zerary(0)`, also `0.z` and `1.u`. From "primary". The seven named ordinals are
    `Prim`, `Sec`, `Ter`, `Quat`, `Quin`, `Sen` and `Sept`; beyond them an ordinal is written
    `7.z` (the eighth) or `8.u`. They are stable values, not inline definitions, so they may be
    used as patterns: `case Prim => …`.

entry .Sec   the second ordinal, `1.z`
entry .Ter   the third ordinal, `2.z`
entry .Quat   the fourth ordinal, `3.z`
entry .Quin   the fifth ordinal, `4.z`
entry .Sen   the sixth ordinal, `5.z`
entry .Sept   the seventh ordinal, `6.z`
    The last of the named ordinals; the eighth is `7.z`. Note that `7.z` is the *eighth*
    position (index 7), while `Sept` is the seventh (index 6).

entry .z   `n.z`: the ordinal at zero-based index `n`, so `0.z` is `Prim`
    An inline extension on `Int`, recording that the number came from a zero-based convention
    — a wire protocol, a JVM array index — at the point it enters. `n.z.n0` is `n` again.

entry .u   `n.u`: the ordinal at one-based count `n`, so `1.u` is `Prim`
    The counterpart of `z` for a one-based convention — a specification's "the 3rd field", a
    line number. `n.u.n1` is `n` again.

entry .nil   `xs.nil`: whether a value is empty, in O(1) for every shape
    Over `Vacuiscible`, which every `Countable` extends: `List`, `Chain`, `Set`, `Map`, `Text`,
    the frozen array, `Option`, a `StringBuilder`, … Never gated: a `List`'s emptiness is one
    pointer test, so `list.nil` needs no acknowledgement where `list.size` does, and
    `!xs.nil` is the idiom the standard library's `nonEmpty` becomes.

entry .size   `xs.size`: the number of elements of any `Countable` value
    O(1) on `Sequence`, `Set`, `Map`, `Ledger`, `Text`, the frozen array and the stdlib
    `IndexedSeq`; on a `List` it is a traversal and resolves only under `import
    dysasymptotics.linearSize`, and on a `Chain` it forces the whole stream and resolves only
    under `unboundedSize`. An `Int` counts itself (`5.size` is `5`), and an `Option` has size
    zero or one.

entry .gamut   `xs.gamut`: the whole range of a countable value as an `Interval`, from `Prim`
    `Interval.initial(xs.size)`: the ordinals `Prim` up to but excluding `xs.limit`. An
    unbranded interval; `extent` is the same range confined to the value, which is what a loop
    wants. Gated as `size` is on a `List` or `Chain`.

entry .extent   `xs.extent`: the whole range of a value as an interval confined to it, `Interval in xs.type`
    The branded `gamut`. Iterating it (`xs.extent.each`, `xs.extent.tabulate`, `xs.extent.all`)
    hands the lambda ordinals typed `Ordinal in xs.type`, and `xs(ordinal)` on such an ordinal
    is total: a bare element, no bounds check, no `Optional`. `capped(n)`, `beyond(n)`, and
    `start thru end` from two confined ordinals narrow it without losing the brand. Sound for
    an immutable receiver on a stable path (a `val`); a mutable countable which shrinks after
    the brand is minted invalidates it, and a receiver which is an expression rather than a
    `val` has no stable path to brand.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val text = t"hello"
    var count = 0

    text.extent.each: ordinal =>
      val char: Char = text(ordinal)   // total: the ordinal is confined to `text`
      if char == 'l' then count += 1
    ```

entry .iterate   `xs.iterate { i => … }`: visits every confined ordinal of a value, or of a confined sub-interval
    The older spelling of `xs.extent.each`, branding through the receiver rather than the
    interval; equivalent, and `xs.iterate(range)` takes an `Interval in xs.type` (from
    `extent.capped`, `prefix`, `thru`) to visit part of it. The lambda's ordinal is `Ordinal
    in xs.type`, so `xs(i)` inside is total. Compiles to a tight integer loop.

entry .tabulate   `xs.tabulate { i => … }` or `range.tabulate { i => … }`: one element per ordinal, as a `Sequence`
    The collecting counterpart of `iterate` and `each`: builds a `Sequence` with one element
    per position, in order, with the lambda's ordinal confined to the value (or to the
    branded interval's value) so reads inside are bare. `array.tabulate { i => array(i) +
    i.n0 }` on `Array(10, 20, 30)` is `Sequence(10, 21, 32)`; `Interval.initial(3).tabulate
    (_.n1)` is `Sequence(1, 2, 3)`. Replaces `zipWithIndex` (a raw `Int`) and a mutable
    builder. Three overloads: over a `Countable`, over a plain `Interval`, and over an
    `Interval in form`.

entry .spot   `xs.spot(p)`: the first confined ordinal satisfying a predicate, or `Unset`
    The safe form of a guarded forward scan whose caller consumes the stopping index: the
    predicate sees `Ordinal in xs.type` and the result is `Optional[Ordinal in xs.type]`, so a
    read inside `let` is total. `xs.spot(after)(p)` resumes the scan from the limit of an
    already-scanned branded interval — the second phase of a two-phase scan. An `Ordinal in
    xs.type` stored from `spot(…).or(…)` keeps its brand, so `xs(index)` remains total.

entry .prefix   `xs.prefix(p)`: the longest leading run of indexes satisfying a predicate, as a confined interval
    The result's `limit` is the stopping index, so `xs.prefix(p)` replaces `while i < size &&
    p(i) do i += 1` loops whose callers then slice or resume from `i`. The two-argument form
    `xs.prefix(after)(p)` *extends* `after` by the next run — cumulative, so that an empty run
    cannot lose its anchor (every empty `Interval` normalises to position zero) and the limit
    is always the resume point. A multi-stage scan reads as consecutive stages:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val text = t"  42;"
    val spaces = text.prefix(text(_) == ' ')               // Interval in text.type: positions 0 and 1
    val digits = text.prefix(spaces)(text(_).isDigit)     // positions 0 to 3, cumulatively
    digits.limit.n0                                        // 4: where the terminator starts
    ```

    Named `prefix` rather than `lead`, which is the complement of `last`.

entry .pare   `xs.pare(floor)(p)`: the interval left after trimming a trailing run, never below `floor` elements
    The trailing-trim shape: `while count > 1 && xs(count - 1) == 0 do count -= 1` becomes
    `xs.pare(1)(xs(_) == 0)`, a confined `Interval in xs.type` from `Prim` to the trimmed
    length.

entry .retrace   `xs.retrace { i => … }`: `iterate` in reverse, from the last confined ordinal down to the first

entry .each   `interval.each { i => … }`: visits every ordinal of an interval, in order
    Two overloads: over a plain `Interval`, the lambda receives a plain `Ordinal`; over a
    confined `Interval in form` (from `extent`, `capped`, `beyond`, `prefix`, or `thru`/`till`
    on confined endpoints) it receives `Ordinal in form`, so a read inside is total and returns
    a bare element. This is the combinator which replaces `var i = 0; while i < xs.length do
    … i += 1`, and it compiles to the same tight loop. A same-named `each` on an unrelated type,
    reached through a companion the file imports, can outrank the confined overload and
    silently strip the brand — the read becomes `Optional` again with no error — which is why
    that name is kept off other receivers.

entry .all   `interval.all(p)`: whether every ordinal satisfies the predicate, stopping at the first that does not
    With the same two overloads as `each`, so `xs.extent.all { i => xs(i) == ys.readUnchecked
    (i.n0) }` reads totally. The replacement for `xs.stdlib.indices.forall`.

entry .thru   `start thru end`: the interval from one ordinal to another, end inclusive
    `Ter thru Sen` is `Ter, Quat, Quin, Sen`. Two ordinals confined to the same value bound an
    interval confined to it — every ordinal between two valid indexes is itself valid — which
    is the only way to build a confined interval that does not start at `Prim`. Both overloads
    are package-level, so that neither outranks the other through the opaque type's implicit
    scope.

entry .till   `start till end`: the interval from one ordinal to another, end exclusive
    `Ter till Sen` is `Ter, Quat, Quin`; `Sec till Sec` is empty. Confined endpoints give a
    confined interval, as with `thru`.

entry .capped   `range.capped(n)`: at most the first `n` ordinals of a confined interval, brand preserved
    A sub-interval of a valid extent is itself valid, so clamping keeps the proof:
    `array.extent.capped(2).each { i => … array(i) … }` reads the first two positions totally.
    A negative `n` is treated as zero.

entry .beyond   `range.beyond(n)`: a confined interval without its first `n` ordinals, brand preserved
    The complement of `capped`; together they bound a window at both ends without an
    `Optional` in the way. Named `beyond` because `skip`, `keep` and `after` are rudiments'
    segment vocabulary and a top-level name in the umbrella may be owned by one module only.

entry .limit   `xs.limit`: the exclusive upper bound of a countable value, the ordinal one past its last element
    `xs.size.z`. Gated as `size` is on a `List` or `Chain`. Not confined: it is not a valid
    index, though it is a safe place for a scan to *start*.

entry .ult   `xs.ult`: the ordinal of the last ("ultimate") element, confined to the value, or `Unset`
    `Optional[Ordinal in xs.type]`, so `xs.ult.let(xs(_))` reads the last element totally. On a
    receiver typed `& Populated` (a `List(a, b)` literal, an `occupied` result) it adapts and
    returns the bare `Ordinal in xs.type`, so `xs(xs.ult)` needs no `let`. An ordinal rather
    than an element: the element is rudiments' `last`. Gated as `size` is on a `List` or
    `Chain`.

entry .pen   `xs.pen`: the ordinal of the penultimate element, confined, or `Unset` if there are fewer than two

entry .ant   `xs.ant`: the ordinal of the antepenultimate element, confined, or `Unset` if there are fewer than three

entry .coextent   `xs.coextent(ys)`: a `Coextent` token if the two values have the same size, else `Unset`
    One brand cannot serve two receivers — `Ordinal in xs.type & Ordinal in ys.type` is not a
    subtype of either — so a parallel read goes through the token instead, which carries the
    one size check and transfers an ordinal between the brands: `shared(i)` turns an `Ordinal
    in xs.type` into an `Ordinal in ys.type`, and `shared.each { (i, j) => … }` iterates with
    an index proven against both.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val left = Array(1, 2, 3)
    val right = Array(10, 20, 30)
    var total = 0

    left.coextent(right).let: shared =>
      shared.each { (i, j) => total += left(i)*right(j) }   // both reads total; total is 140
    ```

entry .aka   `value.aka["label"]`: a value tagged with a label type, for named contextual parameters
    The infix type `subject aka "label"` and the extension which makes one. A lambda written
    `(state aka "state", next aka "next") ?=> …` receives its arguments as contextual values
    which the names `state` and `next` select, which is how `Interval#fuse` and rudiments'
    `fuse` and `each` offer `state`, `next` and `ordinal` without a parameter list. Unwrapped
    with `()`.

entry .Ordinal   a position — first, second, third — as a type distinct from `Int`, with no zeroth
    `Ordinal` is the opaque type `denominative.internal.Ordinal`, exported here, represented
    as the zero-based `Int` it stands for but never interchangeable with one. An `Int` becomes
    an ordinal only by naming its convention — `n.z` for a zero-based index, `n.u` for a
    one-based count, or `Ordinal.zerary(n)` and `Ordinal.uniary(n)` — and an ordinal becomes
    an `Int` again only through `n0` or `n1`, so the convention is written at both crossings
    and an off-by-one error has nowhere to hide. The first seven are named: `Prim`, `Sec`,
    `Ter`, `Quat`, `Quin`, `Sen` and `Sept`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val third: Ordinal = Ter
    third.n0               // 2
    third.n1               // 3
    third + 2              // Quin
    Quin - Ter             // 2: the distance between two ordinals is an Int
    third.next             // Quat
    (Ter thru Quin).size   // 3
    ```

    Arithmetic is through symbolism's `+` and `-`: adding or subtracting an `Int` gives an
    `Ordinal`, subtracting one ordinal from another gives the `Int` distance, and ordinals
    compare with `<`, `<=` and the rest through `Comparable` (or, with no typeclass in the
    way, the inline `le`, `lt`, `ge` and `gt`). All of these live in the opaque type's own
    implicit scope, so they need no import.

    A *confined* ordinal, `Ordinal in xs.type`, is an `Ordinal` whose type records that it
    has been proved to index `xs`: `extent`, `spot`, `prefix`, `ult`, `within` and rudiments'
    `confine` mint them, and reading `xs` at one is total. Arithmetic on a confined ordinal
    is allowed (its branded `+` and `-` are in the companion too), but the result is a plain
    `Ordinal`, since it may have left the proven range; `within` re-confines it.

    An ordinal renders as text only through a `Textualizable` chosen by name from the
    `ordinalTextualizables` package, since no one rendering — `3rd`, `ter`, `2♯` — is right
    everywhere.

entry .Interval   a contiguous range of ordinals, from a start ordinal for a number of positions
    `Interval` is the opaque type `denominative.internal.Interval`, exported here: a start
    `Ordinal` and a size packed into one `Long`, so an interval costs no allocation. It is
    written `Ter thru Sen` (end inclusive), `Ter till Sen` (end exclusive) or `Ter span 3` (a
    start and a size), or built from integers with `Interval.zerary(start, end)`,
    `Interval.sized(start, count)` and `Interval.initial(size)`. An empty interval normalises
    to position zero with size zero, whatever start it was asked for.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val range: Interval = Ter thru Sen
    range.start            // Ter
    range.end              // Sen
    range.limit            // Sept: one past the end
    range.size             // 4
    range.contains(Quin)   // true
    var total = 0
    range.each { ordinal => total += ordinal.n1 }   // total is 18
    ```

    An interval may be *confined*, `Interval in xs.type`, when every ordinal in it is a valid
    index of `xs`: `xs.extent`, `xs.prefix(…)`, `xs.pare(…)(…)`, `capped`, `beyond`, and
    `thru` or `till` between two confined ordinals produce one. Iterating a confined interval
    with `each`, `all` or `tabulate` hands the lambda confined ordinals, so every read inside
    is total. The plain `xs.gamut` is the same range without the brand.

entry .Span   a located range of characters in a source — an offset, a line, lines or an area — in one `Long`
    `Span` is the opaque type `denominative.internal.Span`, exported here: a position in a
    source text, packed into a single `Long` tagged by a `Span.Mode` in its top three bits, so
    that a parser can record a position on every node it builds at no allocation cost. The
    five modes are `Empty` (no position: `Span.empty`, all zero, so an absent span needs no
    `Optional`), `Offset` (a character offset and a length, for sources without lines), `Line`
    (a line, a column and a length within that line), `Lines` (whole lines) and `Area` (a
    start line and column to an end line and column).

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val span: Span = Span.line(3.z, 7.z, 4)
    span.mode          // Span.Mode.Line
    span.startLine     // 3.z
    span.endColumn     // 11.z
    span.length        // 4
    span.offset        // Unset: a line-mode span carries no offset
    ```

    Every accessor which a mode does not carry returns `Unset`. Lines, columns and offsets are
    zero-based ordinals; the field widths limit an `Offset` span to offsets below 2³¹, a `Line`
    span to 2²³ lines and 2¹⁹ columns, and an `Area` to 2²² lines, 2¹⁴ columns and 2¹¹ lines of
    extent. The XML, TEL, YAML and source-code parsers record their nodes' positions as spans.

entry .Coextent   evidence that two values have the same size, transferring a confined ordinal between them
    `Coextent[form1, form2]` is the opaque type `denominative.internal.Coextent`, exported
    here, and represented by the shared size. It is minted only by `xs.coextent(ys)`, which
    makes the one size check and returns `Unset` when the sizes differ. A confined ordinal can
    carry the brand of one value only — `Ordinal in xs.type & Ordinal in ys.type` is not a
    subtype of either — so a parallel read of two values goes through the token: applying it
    to an `Ordinal in xs.type` gives an `Ordinal in ys.type`, `invert` gives the token the
    other way round, and its `each` iterates the shared extent with an ordinal proven against
    each value.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val left = Array(1, 2, 3)
    val right = Array(10, 20, 30)
    var total = 0

    left.coextent(right).let: shared =>
      left.extent.each { i => total += left(i)*right(shared(i)) }   // total is 140
    ```

entry .internal   the opaque representations of `Ordinal`, `Interval`, `Span` and `Coextent`, and their operations
  detail
      The object which defines the four opaque types, so that their representations — `Int`,
      `Long`, `Long` and `Int` — are visible only here. The types are exported to the
      `denominative` package, and their operations are extension methods in this object,
      which is part of each opaque type's implicit scope: a user never names `internal`, and
      `ordinal.n0`, `interval.size` or `span.mode` resolve with no import.

      Branded extensions (taking `Ordinal in form` or `Interval in form`) are deliberately
      *not* here: inside this object an opaque type is its representation, so a refinement of
      it would collapse. `thru`, `till`, `capped`, `beyond`, `each`, `all` and `tabulate` are
      package-level in `denominative` for that reason.
  refer denominative.Ordinal
  refer denominative.Interval
  refer denominative.Span
  refer denominative.Coextent
  entry .Ordinal   the opaque ordinal, represented by its zero-based index
    detail
        The type which `denominative.Ordinal` exports, with its companion: the integer
        constructors and the arithmetic and ordering instances, all in implicit scope.
    entry .zerary   `Ordinal.zerary(n)`: the ordinal at zero-based index `n`, as `n.z` writes it
    entry .uniary   `Ordinal.uniary(n)`: the ordinal at one-based count `n`, as `n.u` writes it
    entry .addable   `ordinal + n`: an `Ordinal` moved forward by an `Int` number of positions
    entry .subtractable   `ordinal - other`: the `Int` distance from one ordinal to another
    entry .subtractable2   `ordinal - n`: an `Ordinal` moved back by an `Int` number of positions
        The result is not clamped: `Prim - 1` is an ordinal before the first, which no
        collection will accept as an index. `previous` clamps at `Prim` instead.
    entry .comparable   ordinals compare by position, as their zero-based indexes do
        Makes `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `max`, `min` and sorting available for ordinals.
    entry .brandedAddable   `ordinal + n` on a confined `Ordinal in form`, giving a plain `Ordinal`
        A typeclass's `Self` is exact, so the unbranded `.addable` is not found for a confined
        receiver; this twin is, and it drops the brand, since arithmetic may leave the proven
        range. Re-confine the result with `within`.
    entry .brandedSubtractable   `ordinal - other` between confined ordinals, giving the `Int` distance
    entry .brandedSubtractable2   `ordinal - n` on a confined `Ordinal in form`, giving a plain `Ordinal`
  entry .Interval   the opaque interval, a start and a size packed into a `Long`
    detail
        The type which `denominative.Interval` exports, with its companion of constructors.
    entry .sized   `Interval.sized(start, count)`: `count` positions from zero-based `start`; empty if `count` ≤ 0
    entry .initial   `Interval.initial(size)`: the first `size` positions, `Prim` onwards
        The range of every valid index of a value of that size, which is how `gamut` is built.
    entry .apply   `Interval(start, end)` from two ordinals, end inclusive; `Interval()` is empty
    entry .zerary   `Interval.zerary(start, end)`: from zero-based `start` to zero-based `end`, end exclusive
  entry .Span   the opaque source span, a mode and its fields packed into a `Long`
    detail
        The type which `denominative.Span` exports, with its companion of constructors. Each
        constructor masks its fields to their widths, so an out-of-range line or column is
        truncated rather than rejected.
    entry .Mode   which fields a `Span` carries: `Empty`, `Offset`, `Line`, `Lines` or `Area`
        An enum, comparable with `==`. `Empty` is absence; `Offset` is a character offset and
        a length; `Line` a line, a column and a length; `Lines` a start line and a line count;
        `Area` a start line and column and an end line and column.
    entry .equality   `CanEqual[Span, Span]`, so two spans compare with `==`
    entry .empty   the span which records no position, all bits zero
    entry .offset   `Span.offset(start, length)`: `length` characters from offset `start`, for a source without lines
    entry .line   `Span.line(line, column, length)`: `length` characters within one line
    entry .lines   `Span.lines(startLine, lineCount)`: a run of whole lines
    entry .area   `Span.area(startLine, startColumn, endLine, endColumn)`: a region across lines
        The end line is stored as a delta from the start, so an end line before the start
        is treated as the start line.
  entry .Coextent   the opaque same-size token, represented by the shared size
    detail
        The type which `denominative.Coextent` exports. Its operations are extensions in its
        companion rather than at package level, because `each` and `size` are already
        package-level names in `denominative`.
    entry .apply   `shared(ordinal)`: an `Ordinal in form1` transferred to an `Ordinal in form2`
    entry .invert   the same token with its two brands swapped, to transfer the other way
    entry .size   the size the two values share
    entry .each   `shared.each { (i, j) => … }`: every shared position, proven against both values
        A tight integer loop; the lambda receives the same position typed once for each
        value, so `left(i)` and `right(j)` are both total.
  entry .span   `start span size`: the interval of `size` positions beginning at `start`
      `Ter span 3` is `Ter thru Quin`. Infix.
  entry .le   whether this ordinal is at or before another, without a typeclass
  entry .lt   whether this ordinal is strictly before another, without a typeclass
  entry .ge   whether this ordinal is at or after another, without a typeclass
  entry .gt   whether this ordinal is strictly after another, without a typeclass
      The four comparisons are inline extensions which compile to a single integer
      comparison, and which work on a confined ordinal as well as a plain one.
  entry .next   the ordinal after this one; on an interval, the ordinal after its end (its `limit`)
  entry .previous   the ordinal before this one, clamped at `Prim`; on an interval, the one before its start
  entry .n0   the ordinal as a zero-based `Int` index: `Prim.n0` is `0`
      The crossing back to integers for an API which counts from zero — a JVM array, a
      `String` offset, a wire format. `n.z.n0 == n`.
  entry .n1   the ordinal as a one-based `Int` count: `Prim.n1` is `1`
      The crossing back to integers for a human-facing number — a line number, "the 3rd
      field". `n.u.n1 == n`.
  entry .subsequent   the interval of `size` positions immediately after an ordinal, or after an interval's end
      `Prim.subsequent(3)` is `Sec thru Quat`.
  entry .preceding   the interval of up to `size` positions immediately before an ordinal or an interval
      Clamped at `Prim`: `Sec.preceding(5)` is just `Prim`.
  entry .within   `ordinal.within(xs)`: the ordinal confined to `xs` if it indexes it, else `Unset`
      The one bounds check that turns a plain `Ordinal` into an `Ordinal in xs.type`, after
      which reads of `xs` at it are total. `xs` needs a `Countable` instance and a stable path
      (a `val`). On a confined ordinal it re-confines to another value.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val array = Array(10, 20, 30)
      Sec.within(array).let(array(_))    // 20
      Sept.within(array).let(array(_))   // Unset
      ```
  entry .start   the first ordinal of an interval
  entry .size   the number of ordinals in an interval
  entry .end   the last ordinal of an interval, inclusive
      For an empty interval this is the ordinal before `Prim`; test `nil` first.
  entry .limit   the ordinal one past an interval's end, exclusive
  entry .contains   whether an ordinal lies within an interval
  entry .fuse   `interval.fuse(initial)(…)`: a fold over an interval with `state` and `next` as contextual names
      The lambda is a context function of `state` (the accumulated value) and `next` (the
      current ordinal), each tagged with `aka` so that the names select them without a
      parameter list: `(Sec span 3).fuse(0)(state + next.n0)` is `1 + 2 + 3`, which is `6`.
  entry .nil   whether an interval is empty
  entry .bits   a span's raw `Long` encoding, for storing or transmitting it
  entry .vacant   whether a span is `Span.empty`, recording no position
  entry .exists   whether a span records a position of any mode
  entry .mode   which of the five `Span.Mode`s a span is in
  entry .startLine   the zero-based line a span starts on, or `Unset` for an `Offset` or `Empty` span
  entry .startColumn   the zero-based column a span starts at, or `Unset` unless it is a `Line` or `Area` span
  entry .endLine   the zero-based line a span ends on, inclusive, or `Unset` for an `Offset` or `Empty` span
  entry .endColumn   the column a span ends at, or `Unset` unless it is a `Line` or `Area` span
      For a `Line` span it is the start column plus the length, so the column just after the
      last character.
  entry .length   a span's length in characters, or `Unset` unless it is an `Offset` or `Line` span
  entry .offset   a span's zero-based character offset, or `Unset` unless it is an `Offset` span
  entry .lineCount   how many lines a span covers, or `Unset` for an `Offset` or `Empty` span
  entry .singleLine   whether a span lies on one line: a `Line` span, or an `Area` within one line

entry .protointernal   the opaque `Tagged` type behind `aka`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `subject aka label` is an alias of `Tagged[subject, label]`, and a
      tagged value is made with `value.aka["label"]` and unwrapped with `()`.
  entry .Tagged   a value tagged at the type level with a label, the representation of `aka`
    detail
        An opaque alias of the value's own type, so tagging costs nothing at runtime.
    entry .apply   wraps a value as a `Tagged` with the given label; what `aka` calls
  entry .apply   `tagged()`: the value inside a `Tagged`, with its own purity restored

entry .Zerary   a pattern which matches an `Int` and binds it as a zero-based `Ordinal`
  detail
      `case Zerary(ordinal) =>` matches any `Int` and binds `ordinal` as `int.z`, which is how
      a match on an integer result — an index returned by a search, for instance — crosses into
      ordinals at the pattern rather than in the case body. The match always succeeds, so it
      is most useful as one case beside `Unset` in a match on an `Optional[Int]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val found: Optional[Int] = 4
      found.asMatchable match
        case Zerary(position) => position.n1   // 5
        case _                => 0
      ```
  entry .Match   the always-succeeding, allocation-free result of `Zerary.unapply`
    detail
        A value class, so a successful match allocates nothing.
    entry #int   the matched integer
    entry #isEmpty   always `false`: the match never fails
    entry #get   the matched integer as a zero-based `Ordinal`
  entry .unapply   matches an `Int`, yielding it as a zero-based `Ordinal`

entry .Dysasymptotic   the marker types which gate an operation whose cost grows with data outside its subject
  detail
      An operation on a data structure is *dysasymptotic* when its cost grows (more than
      logarithmically) with data that is not its subject — the size of the containing
      structure, or the distance of the subject from the point of access — while the subject
      itself stays bounded. `size` on a `List` (the subject is one number, the cost is the
      whole list), `at(n)` on a `List` (one element, a walk of `n`), `size` on a `Chain` (one
      number, a stream forced to its end) are dysasymptotic; `fold` over a `List` is not, since
      its cost tracks its subject, however large. It is a property of the operation on a
      *representation*: `size` is free on a `Sequence`.

      So such an operation's typeclass instance takes one of these marker types as a `using`
      parameter, and a program opts in by importing the matching given from the
      `dysasymptotics` package by name. They are plain markers, not capabilities: they carry
      no authority and constrain nothing but resolution, so every acknowledgement of a hidden
      cost is a greppable import, ideally block-scoped and commented.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def summary(names: List[Text]): Text =
        import dysasymptotics.linearSize   // a short list of names
        t"${names.size} names"
      ```
  refer denominative.dysasymptotics
  entry .LinearSize   the cost of a strict linked structure's length, or a whole rebuild for a bounded change
      Gates `size` on a `List` and everything derived from it (`gamut`, `limit`, `ult`, `pen`,
      `ant`, `extent`, `iterate`, `retrace`), `last` and `lead` on a `List`, and the
      copy-on-write edits of a frozen array (`define`, `omit`, `lead`, and appending or
      prepending an element), each of which copies the whole array. Enabled by
      `dysasymptotics.linearSize`.
  entry .LinearAccess   the cost of reaching a position in a strict linked structure by walking from its head
      Gates positional reads of a `List` (`at`, `apply` and `confine` with an ordinal) and
      `define` on a `List`, which rebuilds its prefix. The head is free: reading it is a
      bounded walk. Enabled by `dysasymptotics.linearAccess`.
  entry .UnboundedSize   the cost of forcing a lazy structure to its end, which never returns on an infinite one
      Gates `size`, `last` and `lead` on a `Chain`. Enabled by `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`.
      Emptiness (`nil`) forces only the first cell and is never gated.
  entry .LinearScan   the cost of finding a subject by scanning a structure which keeps no index for it
      For instance the predecessors of a node in a graph which stores only successors: linear
      in the whole graph however few predecessors there are. Enabled by
      `dysasymptotics.linearScan`.

entry .dysasymptotics   the four acknowledgements of a hidden cost, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Importing one of these givens opens every operation gated on its `Dysasymptotic` marker
      within the import's scope. They must be imported by name — `import
      dysasymptotics.linearSize` — because a wildcard `import dysasymptotics.*` does not bring
      givens into scope. The package merges with the umbrella's own, so `import soundness.*`
      followed by the named import is all that is needed. Scope the import to the block that
      accepts the cost, with a comment saying why it is acceptable; and do not dodge a gate
      with `.stdlib.length` or the like, which hides the same cost where no one can find it.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Ungated: emptiness is O(1) on every shape
        code
            locally:
              val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
              numbers.nil   // false
      frame
        label Gated: the size of a list is a traversal
        code
            locally:
              val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
              numbers.size   // does not compile
      frame
        label Acknowledged in the block that pays for it
        code
            locally:
              import dysasymptotics.linearSize   // three elements
              val numbers: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
              numbers.size   // 3
      ##
      ```
  refer denominative.Dysasymptotic
  entry .linearSize   opens `size` and the size-derived operations on a `List`, and copying edits of a frozen array
  entry .linearAccess   opens positional access into a `List`: `at`, an ordinal `apply`, and `define`
  entry .unboundedSize   opens `size`, `last` and `lead` on a `Chain`, which never return on an infinite one
  entry .linearScan   opens operations which find their subject by scanning an unindexed structure

entry .ordinalTextualizables   the renderings of an `Ordinal` as text, each a given to import by name
  detail
      An `Ordinal` has no rendering in implicit scope, because none is right everywhere: a
      user-facing message wants `3rd`, a debugging trace wants both indexes, a protocol wants
      a bare number. Import exactly one of these givens by name — `import
      ordinalTextualizables.englishOrdinal` — and `ordinal.textual` uses it; a wildcard import does not
      bring givens into scope.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative English
        code
            locally:
              import ordinalTextualizables.englishOrdinal
              Ter.textual   // t"3rd"
      alternative Nominal
        code
            locally:
              import ordinalTextualizables.nominalOrdinal
              Ter.textual   // t"ter"
      alternative Zero-based
        code
            locally:
              import ordinalTextualizables.zeraryOrdinal
              Ter.textual   // t"2♯"
      alternative Both indexes
        code
            locally:
              import ordinalTextualizables.intermediateOrdinal
              Ter.textual   // t"⌞2⌟|⌞3⌟"
      ##
      ```
  entry .nominalOrdinal   the ordinal's name — `prim`, `sec`, … `sept` — and `7.z` beyond the seventh
  entry .uniaryOrdinal   the one-based count marked with `♭`: `Prim` is `1♭`
  entry .zeraryOrdinal   the zero-based index marked with `♯`: `Prim` is `0♯`
  entry .unmarkedUniaryOrdinal   the bare one-based count: `Prim` is `1`
  entry .unmarkedZeraryOrdinal   the bare zero-based index: `Prim` is `0`
  entry .intermediateOrdinal   both indexes at once, zero-based then one-based: `Prim` is `⌞0⌟|⌞1⌟`
  entry .englishOrdinal   the English ordinal suffix: `1st`, `2nd`, `3rd`, `4th`, `11th`, `21st`
  entry .englishSuperscriptOrdinal   the English suffix in superscript: `1ˢᵗ`, `2ⁿᵈ`, `3ʳᵈ`, `4ᵗʰ`
  entry .frenchOrdinal   the French form: `1ᵉʳ`, then `2ᵉ`, `3ᵉ`, …
  entry .italianOrdinal   the Italian form: `1ᵒ`, `2ᵒ`, …
  entry .spanishOrdinal   the Spanish form: `1.ᵒ`, `2.ᵒ`, …
  entry .russianOrdinal   the Russian form: `1-й`, `2-й`, …

entry .Countable   a value with a size, and so a range of ordinals: `Self is Countable`
  detail
      `Countable` is the typeclass behind `size`, and through it behind every operation which
      derives ordinals from a size: `gamut`, `extent`, `limit`, `ult`, `pen`, `ant`, `iterate`,
      `retrace`, `tabulate`, `spot`, `prefix`, `pare`, `coextent` and `within`, and rudiments'
      segment operations. It extends `Vacuiscible`, so a countable value is also testable for
      emptiness, by default as `size == 0`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"hello".size              // 5
      Set(1, 2, 3).size          // 3
      Sequence(1, 2).limit       // Ter
      ```

      Instances exist, ungated, for the frozen array (and its `Sized` form), `Sequence`, `Set`,
      `Map`, `Ledger`, `Text`, the stdlib `IndexedSeq` and `Iterable`, `Option` (zero or one),
      `Int` (which counts itself), and the mutable `ArrayBuffer`, `HashMap`, `TrieMap` and
      `StringBuilder`. A `List`'s size is a traversal, so `.list` needs `import
      dysasymptotics.linearSize`; a `Chain`'s forces the whole stream, so `.chain` needs
      `import dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`. Neither gate applies to `nil`, which `Vacuiscible`
      answers in O(1) for both.

      A confined ordinal minted over a mutable countable (`ArrayBuffer`, `StringBuilder`) is
      valid only while the value does not shrink; the brands are sound for immutable values
      held in a `val`.
  refer denominative.Vacuiscible
  refer denominative.size
  refer denominative.extent
  entry #size   the number of elements in the value
  entry #nil   whether the value is empty; by default, whether its size is zero
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[e]^{}` counts its length, in O(1)
  entry .sizedFrozenArray   a `Sized[length]` frozen array, by its own instance
      A separate instance rather than a widening of `.frozenArray`: a type variable bounded by
      a capturing type would be elaborated as `array^{any.rd}` and fail evidence declared for
      the pure array.
  entry .int   an `Int` counts itself, so `5.extent` is the first five ordinals
  entry .arrayBuffer   a mutable `ArrayBuffer` counts its current length
  entry .option   an `Option` has size zero or one
  entry .list   a `List`'s size, by traversal; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize` in scope
      Takes a `Dysasymptotic.LinearSize` as a `using` parameter, so `list.size`, `list.gamut`,
      `list.extent` and the rest fail to resolve until `import dysasymptotics.linearSize` is
      in scope. Subtype-parametric, so a `List[e] & Populated` matches.
  entry .iterable   any stdlib `Iterable` counts its size, at whatever cost its own `size` has
  entry .map   a `Map` counts its entries, in O(1)
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` counts its entries, in O(1)
  entry .trieMap   a concurrent `TrieMap` counts its entries
  entry .hashMap   a mutable `HashMap` counts its entries
  entry .chain   a `Chain`'s size, by forcing the whole stream; needs `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize` in scope
      On an infinite chain this never returns, which is why the gate is the stronger
      `Dysasymptotic.UnboundedSize` rather than `LinearSize`.
  entry .stringBuilder   a `StringBuilder` counts its current length
  entry .set   a `Set` counts its elements, in O(1)
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq` counts its length, in O(1)
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` counts its elements, in O(1)
  entry .text   a `Text` counts its UTF-16 code units, in O(1)
      The same count as `String#length`: a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane
      counts as two.

entry .Vacuiscible   a value which can be tested for emptiness, in O(1) for every shape: `Self is Vacuiscible`
  detail
      Emptiness alone, split from `Countable` so that a shape whose size is expensive — a
      `List`, a `Chain` — can still answer `nil` cheaply and ungated. An operation which needs
      only to know whether a value is empty should demand `Vacuiscible`, not `Countable`.
      Every `Countable` is `Vacuiscible` through `.countable`; `List` and `Chain` have their
      own instances, which win over the gated ones.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val names: List[Text] = List(t"Ada")
      names.nil                 // false, with no dysasymptotics import
      Chain.from(List(1)).nil   // false: forces only the first cell
      ```
  refer denominative.nil
  refer denominative.Countable
  entry #nil   whether the value has no elements
  entry .countable   every `Countable` is `Vacuiscible`, answering `nil` through its own instance
      Needed because implicit search for `X is Vacuiscible` consults the companions of
      `Vacuiscible` and its parents, never those of its subtypes, so `Countable`'s instances
      would otherwise not be found.
  entry .list   a `List`'s emptiness, one pointer test; ungated, unlike its size
  entry .chain   a `Chain`'s emptiness, forcing only its first cell; ungated, unlike its size

entry .Applicable   a value which can be read at an index or key: `Self is Applicable by Operand to Result`
  detail
      `Applicable` is the read side of indexing: `#contains` says whether an index is valid
      and `#access` reads at a valid one. It is what rudiments' `xs(index)`, `xs.at(index)`,
      `xs.defines(key)`, `xs.confine(index)` and `xs.pick(index)` resolve, and the reason
      those reads can be total for a confined ordinal and `Optional` for any other: `access`
      itself is only ever called behind `contains` or behind a proof. The `Operand` is an
      `Ordinal` for positional shapes and the key type for keyed ones.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val numbers = Sequence(10, 20, 30)
      val ages = Map(t"Ada" -> 36)
      numbers.at(Sec)          // 20
      numbers.at(Sept)         // Unset
      ages.at(t"Ada")          // 36
      ages.defines(t"Bob")     // false
      ```

      Instances: the frozen array and its `Sized` form, `Sequence`, the stdlib `IndexedSeq`
      and `Text` (by `Char`), by ordinal; `Map`, `Ledger` and the mutable `HashMap`, by key;
      and `List`, by ordinal, behind `import dysasymptotics.linearAccess`, since reaching a
      position walks from the head.
  refer denominative.Definable
  refer denominative.Countable
  entry #contains   whether the index is valid for the value: in range, or a present key
  entry #access   the element at an index already known to be valid
      Unchecked: called on an invalid index it may throw. Reach it through rudiments'
      `apply`, `at` or `pick`, which check or prove the index first.
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[e]^{}` read at an ordinal, by a bounds check and an unchecked read
  entry .sizedFrozenArray   a `Sized[length]` frozen array read at a variable ordinal
      A literal index into a `Sized` array is checked at compile time and never reaches this
      instance; a variable ordinal is still a bounds-checked, `Optional` read.
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq` read at an ordinal
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` read at an ordinal, in effectively constant time
  entry .list   a `List` read at an ordinal by walking from its head; needs `dysasymptotics.linearAccess`
  entry .text   a `Text` read at an ordinal, giving a `Char` (a UTF-16 code unit)
  entry .map   a `Map` read at a key, giving its value
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` read at a key, giving its value
  entry .hashMap   a mutable `HashMap` read at a key, giving its value

entry .Definable   copy-on-write update at an index or key: `Self is Definable by Operand to Result`
  detail
      The write twin of `Applicable`, behind rudiments' `xs.define(index, value)`: a copy of
      the value in which reading at `index` gives `value`. It is total: an out-of-range
      ordinal returns the value unchanged, and an absent key adds an entry.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Sequence(1, 2, 3).define(Sec, 20)        // Sequence(1, 20, 3)
      Sequence(1, 2, 3).define(Sept, 20)       // Sequence(1, 2, 3)
      Map(t"a" -> 1).define(t"b", 2)           // Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      ```

      Instances exist for `Sequence`, the stdlib `IndexedSeq`, `Map` and `Ledger`, ungated;
      for `List` behind `dysasymptotics.linearAccess`, since it rebuilds the prefix; and for
      the frozen array behind `dysasymptotics.linearSize`, since every edit copies the whole
      array.
  refer denominative.Applicable
  refer denominative.Omissible
  entry #define   the value with `result` at `index`
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` with one position replaced; unchanged if the ordinal is out of range
  entry .frozenArray   a fresh frozen array with one position replaced; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize`
      Needs a `ClassTag` for the element. The copy is fresh, so freezing it needs no proof.
  entry .list   a `List` with one position replaced, rebuilding its prefix; needs `dysasymptotics.linearAccess`
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq` with one position replaced
  entry .map   a `Map` with a key set to a value, added if absent
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` with a key set to a value; a new key goes at the end of the order

entry .Omissible   copy-on-write removal at an index or key: `Self is Omissible by Operand`
  detail
      The inverse of `define`, behind rudiments' `xs.omit(index)`. It is a typeclass of its
      own because linked and sequential shapes cannot offer it honestly: removing a position
      shifts every later index, so `List` and `Sequence` have no instance, and `omit` does not
      compile on them. The frozen array is the exception, since every edit of it copies the
      whole array anyway, so removing a slot costs what `define` does; it is gated on the same
      `dysasymptotics.linearSize`. An out-of-range ordinal, or an absent key, returns the value
      unchanged.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2).omit(t"a")   // Map(t"b" -> 2)
      ```
  refer denominative.Definable
  entry #omit   the value without the element at `index`
  entry .frozenArray   a fresh frozen array one slot shorter; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize` and a `ClassTag`
  entry .map   a `Map` without a key
  entry .ledger   a `Ledger` without a key, the remaining order kept

entry .Terminable   a value whose last element can be read: `Self is Terminable by Operand`
  detail
      The back end of a value, behind rudiments' `xs.last` (which returns `Unset` on an empty
      value, and a bare element on one typed `& Populated`). A separate typeclass from
      `Truncable` because the two costs differ: an array's last element is one read, while
      dropping it copies the array. `last` is gated wherever it is not a peek.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Sequence(1, 2, 3).last   // 3
      t"hello".last            // 'o'
      ```

      Instances: `Sequence`, the frozen array and its `Sized` form, `Text` (by `Char`) and the
      stdlib `IndexedSeq`, in O(1); `List` behind `dysasymptotics.linearSize`, since it walks
      to the end; and `Chain` behind `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`, since it forces the whole
      stream.
  refer denominative.Truncable
  entry #last   the last element of a non-empty value
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence`'s last element, in O(1)
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen array's last slot, in O(1)
  entry .sizedFrozenArray   a `Sized[length]` frozen array's last slot, by its own instance
  entry .text   a `Text`'s last `Char`
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq`'s last element
  entry .list   a `List`'s last element, by walking to its end; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize`
  entry .chain   a `Chain`'s last element, by forcing it to its end; needs `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`

entry .Truncable   a value which can drop its last element: `Self is Truncable to Result`
  detail
      `last`'s complement, behind rudiments' `xs.lead`: everything but the last element, so
      that `lead` followed by `last` is the whole value. The `Result` is a plain type rather
      than `Self`, because dropping an element may empty the value and a `& Populated` brand
      must not survive.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Sequence(1, 2, 3).lead   // Sequence(1, 2)
      t"hello".lead            // t"hell"
      ```

      Instances: `Sequence` (amortised O(1)), `Text` and the stdlib `IndexedSeq`, ungated;
      `List` and the frozen array behind `dysasymptotics.linearSize`, since each copies its
      whole structure; and `Chain` behind `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`.
  refer denominative.Terminable
  entry #lead   the value without its last element
  entry .sequence   a `Sequence` without its last element, in amortised O(1)
  entry .text   a `Text` without its last `Char`
  entry .indexedSeq   a stdlib `IndexedSeq` without its last element
  entry .list   a `List` without its last element, copying its spine; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize`
  entry .frozenArray   a fresh frozen array one slot shorter; needs `dysasymptotics.linearSize` and a `ClassTag`
      An empty array truncates to an empty array.
  entry .chain   a `Chain` without its last element; needs `dysasymptotics.unboundedSize`
